The error occurs because your RedisTemplate is defined as RedisTemplate<String, byte[]>. This means it **only accepts byte[]** as the value, but you are trying to pass a String (scheduledTime).
To fix this, you have two options. The cleanest way is to convert your String to bytes.
### The Fix
Change your set call to convert the string into a byte array:
```java
// 1. Convert the String to a byte array using UTF-8
byte[] scheduledTimeBytes = scheduledTime.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

// 2. Pass the byte array to Redis
redisTemplate.opsForValue().set("META_SCHEDULE_" + reqId, scheduledTimeBytes, 1, TimeUnit.HOURS);

```
### Why this is happening:
 * Your RedisTemplate<String, byte[]> is configured as a ByteArrayRedisTemplate.
 * Spring Data Redis forces strict type-checking on the value part of the template.
 * By using getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8), you are properly serializing your data into the format the RedisTemplate expects.
### Pro-Tip for Retrieval
When you need to read this back in your VoucherRequestServiceImpl, you will need to convert the bytes back to a String:
```java
// Retrieve from Redis
byte[] data = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("META_SCHEDULE_" + requestId);

// Convert back to String
String scheduleStr = (data != null) ? new String(data, StandardCharsets.UTF_8) : null;

```
This approach maintains the integrity of your current RedisTemplate configuration without needing to change your entire service structure!
